{# output_language.prompt — the one line that asks for content in the player's
   language. Included at the end of each prompt whose answer has content
   fields: text the player reads, or that becomes a memory. Reads
   `output_language` (the language's English name), `content_fields` (the
   answer's fields to write in it) and `prompt_forms` ([{ field, form }]: the
   shape the language file asks of a field one of its templates splices in),
   which C++ sets (see PromptLanguage.h). Every other field is a token and
   keeps its values, and a name keeps its spelling, and its alphabet, even
   inside content: the matchers find people by the names the game gives
   them, and a memory that writes one person two ways reads as two people. Renders nothing
   for English, so an English prompt is unchanged. #}{% if output_language != "English" %}
Write {% for f in content_fields %}{% if not loop.is_first %}{% if loop.is_last %} and {% else %}, {% endif %}{% endif %}`{{ f }}`{% endfor %} in {{ output_language }}.{% for p in prompt_forms %} Write `{{ p.field }}` as {{ p.form }}.{% endfor %} Every other field keeps the exact values described above. Never translate or transliterate the name of a person, place or faction: write it in the spelling and script this prompt uses. An epithet such as "the Fair" or "the Younger" is part of the name. A name keeps the alphabet this prompt writes it in, in every field: one written here in Latin letters stays in Latin letters, letter for letter, inside {% for f in content_fields %}{% if not loop.is_first %}{% if loop.is_last %} and {% else %}, {% endif %}{% endif %}`{{ f }}`{% endfor %} too. Only a name this prompt already writes in {{ output_language }}'s own letters may take, inside those fields, the ending {{ output_language }} grammar requires; everywhere else a name is copied exactly as written. Everything else is {{ output_language }}: a title, a rank, a school of magic or a common noun (a jarl, a steward, a master wizard, conjuration, imperial, a library) is translated like any other word.{% endif -%}
